<p>President Trump said he would support Iranian Kurdish fighters launching a ground offensive into Iran, casting it as part of the expanding US-Israeli conflict with the Islamic Republic. Several outlets also reported that Trump discussed possible US support, including “extensive air cover,” as Kurdish groups in northern Iraq prepare for potential cross-border operations. His comments come as regional strikes appear to be intensifying. Israel says it has begun a “broad-scale” wave of attacks on infrastructure targets in Tehran, while Gulf cities face renewed Iranian bombardment; Azerbaijan, meanwhile, warned it would retaliate after Iranian missiles hit its territory—signs of a conflict spreading beyond its original fronts. Separate reporting describes the Kurds as a large, dispersed ethnic group across Iran, Iraq, Syria, and Turkey, with a long history of seeking greater autonomy—context that shapes both the potential military role and the political risks of opening a new Kurdish front inside Iran.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/GE5U2WJsX2O5XdIz3XFG9m2xta236L5LhyCxXif8TAVkUAbHU3NJHRYjke_a7LWlxdqCHVhTo8VRDxULqqw84NXpUTsL7jtMWcb3Ja-581Wc9LUiFMZqlSAx9ALVOSi3yhbBzOLCV0msq0iuh7c6DhajksF9bKlHPfqM6poVjpllLyqbmkUyvjWkLLO-Wgy6ij3TiAChk4X42HuHc15BcPKQ7g14eKRBA4ZFxqKfxd_Kcdky6dBQWaJVc5FCH7uLfuDNZs0qk-2GZPLN7w' alt='FILE PHOTO: A fighter from the Kurdistan Freedom Party (PAK) takes part in a training exercise at a base near Erbil, Iraq, February 12, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Air cover offer: The Telegraph said Trump told reporters the US Air Force would provide “extensive air cover” for Kurdish forces if they launch a ground offensive against Iran’s government.</li><li>Leader question: The Straits Times reported Trump said he wants a say in who leads Iran next as the conflict intensifies, alongside encouraging Iranian Kurdish forces to go on the offensive.</li><li>Cross-border claims: AllSides summarized accounts that hundreds—possibly thousands—of Kurdish fighters had moved into Iran near the Iraqi border, though officials denied the reports.</li><li>Kurdish coalition: Le Monde reported Iranian Kurdish units have formed an unusually united coalition, while warning that Kurdish alliances have often proved fragile and shaped by betrayals.</li><li>Who the Kurds are: The New York Times said Kurds number about 40 million across several countries and have long sought greater autonomy—important backdrop to any potential push into Iran.</li></ul><blockquote>I think it’s wonderful. - Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Donald Trump: He said he would be “all for” a Kurdish ground assault into Iran and praised the idea of an offensive. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/5/trump-voices-support-for-possible-kurdish-offensive-in-iran'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Israel: Israel said it has started a “broad-scale” wave of attacks on infrastructure targets in Tehran. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/trump-urges-iranian-kurds-attack-iran-war-widens'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Azerbaijan: Azerbaijan warned it would retaliate after Iranian missiles struck its territory. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/trump-urges-iranian-kurds-attack-iran-war-widens'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Iranian Kurdish factions (as analyzed): Le Monde described rising hope among some observers that Kurdish factions could pressure Iran’s system from within, while stressing the risks created by past broken alliances. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/03/05/kurdish-factions-could-weaken-iran-s-regime-from-within_6751139_4.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/i-think-its-wonderful-trump-backs-possible-kurdish-offensive-in-iran-11175632#publisher=newsstand'>&quot;I Think It&#x27;s Wonderful&quot;: Trump Backs Possible Kurdish Offensive In Iran</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com